  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 129 PM MST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nThe majority of the long term will feature relatively benign weather \nwith a return to more seasonable temperatures and breezy\nconditions.\n\nA pattern change will arrive on Wednesday as a \ntrough swings into the Rockies. A cold front will move across \nthe area, late Tuesday night or early Wednesday morning, leading\nto high temperatures in the 40s and 50s--roughly average for \nthis time of year. This front will be mostly dry, with \npredominately west winds promoting a dry downslope environment. \nThe mountains, however, could see a quick shot of snow, with the\nSierra Madre Range picking up a few inches of accumulation. The\nSnowys will likely be shadowed and not see much in the way of \naccumulations. The bigger concern with the frontal passage will \nbe strong winds. Marginal high winds will be possible as MSLP \ngradients compress from the front. Winds aloft will increase up \nto about 50 kts with decent subsidence, so cannot rule out 60 \nMPH gusts during the day in the typical southeast Wyoming wind \nprones.\n\nSeasonal temperatures will stick around on Thursday with little to \nno weather impacts expected. Some lingering breezy conditions are \nstill likely west of the Laramie Range on Thursday, but the rest of \nthe forecast area will see a quiet day. Friday into the weekend, \nthings could get interesting with a potential impactful winter \nstorm. Of course, its still over a week away with plenty of time for \nmodels to come to a solution, but there is a chance for accumulating \nsnow and windy conditions.",
